#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175701 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "e65b269e-a33e-5bc6-b13a-c3f12488f260" :question/math-academy-id "e-5527" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/6e4a720878567b81cb593aa5a9bfc5aa2c8c7ace61d111bb8934bc94056c04bc.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3+4\\textrm{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-3+4\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-3+4\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\textrm{i}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/0d2e7b9f7011624cf74762419efd784144cf41e2000301d093e1aa612e96f495.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/2e4bc467e9fbc15574d2739766eada3f297500ecf1a69382500271751b2edf65.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/id #uuid "acaa8e8f-de36-56b0-8e02-ded7fd08118c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5528" :question/problem "Determine whether ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/59af73d1cda1a988c775ccf198b53653b96660b4ad8416add1b0c1816e89a5a5.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/963bcaf94bc481fe8fbc99ca28fba037047d8821661eadcf0e5e9ce50ba39ef8.png)\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 24\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v} = 6\\textrm{i} \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=6\\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "da3e8948-1f17-5281-a2f7-2fb1732939d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5529" :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/07f01e2a202e29fee28a98d386df1d22fd21efcf1ee4d0a6a4cc56f9b637d57c.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/a547975b3524322daec753a8ddd010e50e815307d72bc07933d1e3674b4b462c.png) be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/53de45d6b1504ec53ca81471731b06ac5524a12230f0414553624c116a6f47c6.png)\nGiven that $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of ${\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =4+5\\textrm{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5\\text{i}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-5\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(4+5\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = \\textrm{i}x_1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/b212192b142e9b4686a4d39e5957db5f3bfbca6283bfcb1d88a6fc57a4a27848.png)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/65c073a60a27234349281e873958c2c9d54fb703331a4de23cc358a3eab1b232.png) \n\nNow, since $\\lambda_1=4+5\\textrm{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda_1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=\\overline{\\lambda_1}=4-5\\textrm{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf v_1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070616/assets/ca11ae0cca8d07c026108e71b96398171597f7330cb2390d871a8071e7bba873.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\dfrac{a_1a_2}{b_1b_2}} = {\\dfrac{(1)(1)}{(\\textrm{i})(-\\textrm{i})}} = 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "494ad08e-b220-5b32-bd03-23643745c2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367814} {:answer/id #uuid "ee79e0e0-6a8c-502c-ac27-a4913d40a7b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367815} {:answer/id #uuid "8189ed53-deac-56bc-8250-a321f067f001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367816} {:answer/id #uuid "0cbb9d12-4ae9-5a48-a17b-3a75f45dcfe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367817} {:answer/id #uuid "3cadc242-f649-541a-b484-089d5bdd4141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3+4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cbb9d12-4ae9-5a48-a17b-3a75f45dcfe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367817} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd077df7-8220-5b57-82cf-62aead5b0794" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfeca104-fe47-5c0d-8612-60fbd83f3f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-140383" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{\\text{i}}^{2}+4\\text{i} \\\\ -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}(4+3\\text{i}) \\\\ (-1)(4+3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4+3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4+3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c16cf0e8-24d1-5fd8-82bd-09e4bc7de781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367821} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0e595d-bda3-5307-9825-4fbe80444ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367822} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1032ae-603d-5980-b664-73e9368ee165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =3+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367823} {:answer/id #uuid "cf87e6c2-102f-51a9-88df-2b42a0d33172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367824} {:answer/id #uuid "f02cf6b9-7107-5597-b97b-8a9b67442511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f02cf6b9-7107-5597-b97b-8a9b67442511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367825} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd0b0847-d6de-549b-ae6c-60ea1caec4e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a27e7a9-e009-56bc-8df0-b8ef241b7ee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140384" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}+6\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}(2-3\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(2-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-3\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(2-3\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b54a1906-4418-558c-9b9c-70f6e198cd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343580} {:answer/id #uuid "0b44b287-f452-5531-9f51-50420157ee60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343581} {:answer/id #uuid "9845e8b3-3a41-5521-88bc-2a40aafd1faf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343582} {:answer/id #uuid "a6dbe8dd-0ad3-5585-a145-aa06f0e310bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343583} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd60022-6510-51e0-ae95-18db32723735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b54a1906-4418-558c-9b9c-70f6e198cd44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343580} :answer-field/id #uuid "285c6789-0ea2-580c-8c00-303d243386d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54f8d78f-9334-58cc-8d51-450e53ee811c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140385" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9fa9dc7-1985-50e8-ba92-8c4f0c74195a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367828} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ffd505-0aca-55df-b883-063397d6cc1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367829} {:answer/id #uuid "ae094918-bda3-50d6-8989-ac52320024ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367830} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7405f3-c68a-5000-b8c2-43c6e21a5bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367831} {:answer/id #uuid "404b51f2-4d65-579b-8196-889dc878b3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "404b51f2-4d65-579b-8196-889dc878b3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367832} :answer-field/id #uuid "d491997a-9175-5259-809d-b95e13173eb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01a38607-b587-5299-b88e-717f9d67686a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140386" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 2 & 3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(3+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(-3+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3+\\text{i}}{5}=-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1525b61-1791-537e-a7c9-ed078a59c211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313259} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1e9fdf-66ef-5e8f-8c7d-5bec55e8637d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313260} {:answer/id #uuid "a6548834-4797-5767-916e-b73981f8869b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313261} {:answer/id #uuid "59255893-b8cf-5491-82ad-a934f5ad64b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313262} {:answer/id #uuid "edfb78f9-9410-5c9e-8ebf-b7ae1a265f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1525b61-1791-537e-a7c9-ed078a59c211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313259} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb31aa7-cb8b-575a-b421-246f08ecb24d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98eb69e4-fc5d-5211-9182-b032cfbab6b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140387" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+5\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5-5\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 10 & 5-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-5-5\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}+(5-5\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+5\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+5\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1-5\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}{(-2)(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f1dd336-9ead-5c21-ac6f-768a4eb24855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174901} {:answer/id #uuid "961a2918-8746-55b0-9f8a-3e0bf9ef7e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174902} {:answer/id #uuid "ba70b2fc-ceaa-5d8c-92ef-0cd4b5cd381f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174903} {:answer/id #uuid "c823c9ff-fca6-5959-89d1-86a2418f9e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ =-3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174904} {:answer/id #uuid "6cacf0bc-517f-5104-82fa-779e5d09c203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = \\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f1dd336-9ead-5c21-ac6f-768a4eb24855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ = 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174901} :answer-field/id #uuid "733f9f3a-1888-5241-8b1b-05a81df288bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6beb736-d02a-5b38-8238-42457ab554c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-38475" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ 6-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\text{i} \\\\ -6{\\text{i}}^{2}-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=3\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =3\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c18520c3-2049-51c7-8fe5-765c1abe198a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174919} {:answer/id #uuid "db908152-aff0-5533-ab91-4b47d766321f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174920} {:answer/id #uuid "13d3d5e2-31cf-5838-803e-e3a849c8ef8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174921} {:answer/id #uuid "1d33a334-e204-5efd-b71c-1deeeaf61aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174922} {:answer/id #uuid "45f41a32-8463-5143-8a14-060c4971deb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13d3d5e2-31cf-5838-803e-e3a849c8ef8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174921} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f3a2ce-5a9b-5da7-b976-ffe3fc139874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b212f7d8-65d4-5b14-984d-a9ab26791d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-38480" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ -12+6\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2-2\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(-2-\\text{i})(-2+\\text{i})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af9ebaba-2a47-5692-bc69-a8f2fce7692b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174925} {:answer/id #uuid "e76bab4f-7a1f-5d86-9013-ff4dcf48c4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174926} {:answer/id #uuid "eb710a43-0485-58b7-96ae-5ae89e2cf24a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174927} {:answer/id #uuid "f5be5a2e-e4bd-518a-b046-e5ec38767bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174928} {:answer/id #uuid "06b8ee26-deaf-5dfa-aee7-3d12fb9184c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{5}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb710a43-0485-58b7-96ae-5ae89e2cf24a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174927} :answer-field/id #uuid "404d9eef-cae6-54bf-871d-717e017b4530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "795927e9-7dd6-5d7f-82cf-cc21ad14ed8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39185" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10+2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -26 & -10+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(10+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -26{x}_{1}+(-10+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A+2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(5+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{5+\\text{i}}{-2}=-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8d01964-7f9e-59fa-aa7e-073f5ce56aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367835} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3fcf41-b2f5-59d7-af80-98ca2e37a9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367836} {:answer/id #uuid "86b113d6-a6a1-5ec7-88b2-0d4d15cb2999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367837} {:answer/id #uuid "595fdd39-4f69-5e94-be96-018923aa641d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367838} {:answer/id #uuid "47502250-b107-585d-906b-4b32c34377fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47502250-b107-585d-906b-4b32c34377fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367839} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f35160-927e-5f50-9e29-b16c734a2eca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ce55420-8f30-5a2d-834c-83df9e2c32db" :question/math-academy-id "q-39187" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6+3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -15 & -6+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -15{x}_{1}+(-6+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{\\text{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c77030-9cf4-5578-966b-acfc2fa9adec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367842} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b85246-0ab6-5181-9632-508cce9a36e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367843} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddd3b59-8223-5bf0-bd2a-3e8718ebea03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367844} {:answer/id #uuid "25535043-dea9-5459-998d-edcb9baf3623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367845} {:answer/id #uuid "46c68393-3e48-5ce6-8018-cc90f4b27a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25535043-dea9-5459-998d-edcb9baf3623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367845} :answer-field/id #uuid "14560430-1af1-5053-a3fd-ec6331811b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62f097b5-cd5d-5f7e-878a-f8d5552fa6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39188" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8+4\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-8+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+(8+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-2+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=4\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2+\\text{i})(-2-\\text{i})}{(1)(1)}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b7e5abb-ba1b-5694-a51c-00b3f4716155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313294} {:answer/id #uuid "ed430d33-301c-5b50-9e48-bc43b4bb69a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313295} {:answer/id #uuid "293fa2ab-af7e-5899-bffd-fa294af061a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313296} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a088f0-e7a8-5b56-a1b8-b6f6b6d67000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313297} {:answer/id #uuid "578dbc7e-f6b3-5363-8f36-12dae84e5935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed430d33-301c-5b50-9e48-bc43b4bb69a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313295} :answer-field/id #uuid "4de5a998-ba1c-51f3-b9a1-49e7e489c1d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88f59971-0e5e-53c1-83ea-ae2fc365b9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81024" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\text{i}}^{2}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i}(\\text{i}+1) \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(1+\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbfab196-fbbe-5bbb-8882-73deacfad9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313301} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d65a70-d39e-5836-a745-ff424454a9a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313302} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb3537c-94f8-56d2-a6c3-ea4f0dce3269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313303} {:answer/id #uuid "ba227c4e-9079-5da3-86c2-30143361bd29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313304} {:answer/id #uuid "acc4784c-0bd0-5238-ac92-d37b8a309280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cb3537c-94f8-56d2-a6c3-ea4f0dce3269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda =-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313303} :answer-field/id #uuid "843ce327-8729-57ce-8689-9455632a4002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b512857-cfb0-5733-8d7f-8d3249677209" :question/math-academy-id "q-81025" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-4\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0192428f-d96f-526b-a0c5-e2b086a252b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343608} {:answer/id #uuid "8e09cb6b-4880-5e9e-93c9-49781e63881b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343609} {:answer/id #uuid "d271ba02-9a28-51b9-a7ae-7eaddc5376a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343610} {:answer/id #uuid "87a3be2a-00dc-523a-a60e-e07812fe916a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343611} {:answer/id #uuid "3411e5dd-6676-512f-b57b-f7685ed2caf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e09cb6b-4880-5e9e-93c9-49781e63881b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343609} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5f6cc44-b56b-56a9-84f3-a6bde3a09977" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c27b7b8-e30b-536b-a69b-9501e33a6b11" :question/math-academy-id "q-81184" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b431d46-4be7-5db9-8c73-03f3c9fe89fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313336} {:answer/id #uuid "e84a0c7e-6bc8-50dd-b60b-80e86d74aef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313337} {:answer/id #uuid "94a82007-a00a-5d0a-bab3-33bb92f9d37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313338} {:answer/id #uuid "10bac6b1-60fc-5850-8080-5ca503a1cc18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313339} {:answer/id #uuid "e190f602-b6ad-5288-8022-71c00a77c9c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b431d46-4be7-5db9-8c73-03f3c9fe89fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313336} :answer-field/id #uuid "0175c13b-88c7-585a-b8db-46e9f9877777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e94b55c-d751-5642-a64a-58a596312cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81187" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1+3\\text{i}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fffe5aa-ee8a-5b26-8ade-c9c24234c182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367849} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8805f9-1c6e-521c-a98a-cc0f79fe6a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367850} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6a9dad-8386-5818-983d-4906ea5a545f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367851} {:answer/id #uuid "b212765d-6088-51dd-bcb2-b84794cb59e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367852} {:answer/id #uuid "b36e4cb5-c904-56fb-a24e-dd76af5a2e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fffe5aa-ee8a-5b26-8ade-c9c24234c182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367849} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6d9648-4bd5-5a09-8c96-d66b096cb920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "defa1cb1-365b-5e5f-ab67-ca3d065551d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81190" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-3\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"}]}
